const e='<h2>Why a PDF, and why full screen matters</h2>\n\n  <p>A PDF is the most reliable presentation format there is. It doesn\'t need an account, a signal, or the room\'s Wi-Fi to hold up. Your fonts are embedded. Nothing reflows. Export your deck to PDF before you leave and the only variable left is the cable.</p>\n\n  <p>The full-screen part is what stock iOS won\'t give you. Mirror an iPhone to a projector and you get a phone-shaped picture floating in the middle of the screen with black down both sides, your slides end up occupying maybe half the wall. That\'s a shape mismatch between a 19.5:9 phone and a 16:9 projector, and no setting in iOS fixes it, because iOS is copying the phone rather than drawing for the projector.</p>\n\n  <h2>The setup</h2>\n\n  <p><strong>XeOS: External Display Browser</strong> renders its own desktop at the projector\'s resolution, and every window has a full-screen button that strips away all interface. A slide fills the wall.</p>\n\n  <h3>1. Get the PDF onto the device</h3>\n\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Import</strong> from the iOS Files picker, using the Files app\'s import button.</li>\n    <li><strong>Mount a folder</strong>, an iCloud Drive folder, a folder on the phone, or a USB stick in your adapter, and open the file in place. See <a href="/guides/mount-icloud-local-and-external-drives">the drives guide</a>.</li>\n    <li><strong>Download</strong> it from email or the web.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h3>2. Connect to the projector</h3>\n\n  <div class="table-scroll">\n    <table>\n      <thead><tr><th>Room has</th><th>You need</th></tr></thead>\n      <tbody>\n        <tr><td>HDMI, USB-C iPhone</td><td>USB-C to HDMI cable, or a multiport adapter</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>HDMI, Lightning iPhone</td><td>Lightning Digital AV Adapter</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>VGA only (older rooms)</td><td>Lightning to VGA Adapter</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>An Apple TV or AirPlay screen</td><td>Nothing, mirror from Control Centre</td></tr>\n      </tbody>\n    </table>\n  </div>\n\n  <p>Carry a short HDMI cable. Rooms have the port; the cable is usually plugged into something else.</p>\n\n  <h3>3. Open the PDF</h3>\n\n  <p>Double-click it in Files. The built-in viewer opens, with page thumbnails, zoom, search and annotation tools.</p>\n\n  <figure>\n    <img src="/content/images/pdf-viewer.webp" alt="The PDF viewer showing a presentation slide with a toolbar of view, annotate, shapes, insert, form and redact tools." width="1600" height="1006" loading="lazy" decoding="async">\n    <figcaption>The viewer, windowed.</figcaption>\n  </figure>\n\n  <h3>4. Go full screen</h3>\n\n  <p>Click the green button in the window\'s corner.</p>\n\n  <figure class="is-inline">\n    <img src="/content/images/traffic-lights.webp" alt="The four window buttons, with the green full-screen button third from left." width="352" height="118" loading="lazy" decoding="async">\n    <figcaption>The green button removes the title bar, the toolbar and the dock.</figcaption>\n  </figure>\n\n  <figure>\n    <span class="device-frame"><svg class="device-frame-chrome" viewBox="0 0 320 200" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><rect x="0" y="0" width="320" height="184" rx="3" class="device-frame-bezel"/><path d="M146 184 L141 197 L179 197 L174 184 Z" class="device-frame-neck"/><rect x="118" y="196" width="84" height="4" rx="2" class="device-frame-base"/></svg><span class="device-frame-screen"><img src="/content/images/pdf-fullscreen.webp" alt="A presentation slide filling an entire external display, edge to edge." width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></span></span>\n    <figcaption>The slide on the wall, nothing else on screen.</figcaption>\n  </figure>\n\n  <p>Page through with arrow keys on a Bluetooth keyboard, Page Up / Page Down, a mouse wheel, or a swipe on the phone-as-trackpad. Move the pointer to the top edge to bring the title bar back at the end.</p>\n\n  <h2>Getting the slide to fill the frame</h2>\n\n  <p>Decks exported at 16:9 match most projectors exactly. A 4:3 deck will have a band each side, that\'s the deck\'s shape, not the app\'s. Two things to check if there\'s still a border:</p>\n\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>The projector\'s own picture-size setting</strong>, "Just Scan", "Screen Fit" or "1:1". Overscan is on by default on a lot of hardware.</li>\n    <li><strong>Settings › Display</strong>, pick the resolution the projector reports. Older projectors are happiest at 1280 × 720 or 1920 × 1080.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h2>The other route: Google Slides</h2>\n\n  <p>If the room has Wi-Fi and your deck lives in Google Slides, the browser gives you the real desktop site with its own presenting controls. Open the deck, press Present, then the green button. Full walkthrough in <a href="/guides/present-without-a-laptop">the presenting guide</a>.</p>\n\n  <p>Use it when you have connectivity. Use the PDF when you can\'t rely on it, which, in a school hall or a client\'s meeting room, is most of the time.</p>\n\n  <h2>Pre-flight checklist</h2>\n\n  <ul>\n    <li>Deck exported to PDF and on the device.</li>\n    <li>Adapter <em>and</em> a short HDMI cable.</li>\n    <li>Multiport adapter if the session runs long, so the phone charges.</li>\n    <li>Do Not Disturb on, a message on a four-metre screen is memorable for the wrong reason.</li>\n    <li>Auto-lock off in iOS Settings › Display &amp; Brightness.</li>\n    <li>Content scale checked, so the back of the room can read it.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <div class="cta">\n    <p><strong>XeOS: External Display Browser</strong> turns an iPhone or iPad into a full desktop on any monitor, TV or projector.</p>\n    <p><a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/external-display-browser/id6758286241" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="app-store-link">Download XeOS on the App Store</a></p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class="faq">\n    <h2>Frequently asked</h2>\n\n    <h3>Do I need internet?</h3>\n    <p>Not for the PDF route. The file and the app are both on the device.</p>\n\n    <h3>Can I annotate during the talk?</h3>\n    <p>Yes, the viewer has annotation tools, and changes can be saved back to the file.</p>\n\n    <h3>Can I present a PowerPoint file directly?</h3>\n    <p>There\'s a preview for Office files that\'s good enough to present from, but PDF is the safer bet when exact layout matters.</p>\n\n    <h3>Does this work on an iPad?</h3>\n    <p>Yes, on any iPad that can output video, no Stage Manager needed.</p>\n\n    <h3>Can I see my notes on the phone?</h3>\n    <p>Not as a separate presenter view; the phone acts as your trackpad. Notes on paper or on a second device is the practical answer.</p>\n  </div>\n';export{e as default};
//# sourceMappingURL=present-pdf-fullscreen-from-iphone-to-projector-ByrWGU6k.js.map
